[PLAN] feat.compile.impl: `#impl<f>` (design principles 10.4): a function's Camel body is its semantics and the body-less declaration f of the same module is the operator that runs it. An ordinary call is one OPER of the kernel, the body is not inlined and is absent from the ordinary dump; the body graph stays the semantic function for what asks to look inside (Program::implBodies / test::impl). The agreement case compiles the same function twice in one program, once as a direct call (the kernel) and once through its function value (the body), and checks that both print the same values while a profile counts the two paths apart; a deliberately wrong kernel shows that the comparison can fail. Two production passes are taken through both paths: `std::opt` runs over a program whose tagged function is called both ways and both values stay 42 on every back end, and the reverse pass differentiates a direct call of a tagged function as the body the tag kept, a kernel whose registered rule differs from the body's derivative included. Fusion and the ONNX exporter have no case here for a reason that is a decision rather than effort: both need a kernel over tensors to say anything at all (the `rules::fusion` library's only tensor rule is the sigmoid chain; the exporter requires an exported function's arguments to be tensors, `modules/onnx/exporter.cpp#InputBinder::bind`), and a test module cannot declare a tensor kernel, because a module may declare only its own operator (SemanticDiag::NativeDeclarationForeignOperator). Seven refusals, one case each: the tag on a body-less declaration, a kernel no declaration brings, a kernel of another module, a kernel whose signature differs, a kernel that has a body, a generic function, whose instances carry no single signature, and the one the reverse pass adds, a kernel two of the program's bodies answer for, where a call site cannot say which body it means. Each refusal names the misuse and is pinned to its source line and column.
