[PLAN] feat.modules.tensor.dtype: A tensor's element type is the one the program writes (design/arrays-memory, P4 "element type exact"): Tensor<f64> holds f64, Tensor<i32> holds i32 and Tensor<bf16>/Tensor<f16> hold two bytes of their own reduced type end to end — creation, arithmetic, reduction, to_bytes/from_bytes — with no storage dtype a written type is replaced with. A conversion into bf16 or f16 rounds to nearest, ties to even, once; one the program writes needs no numeric permission, because `change_precision` governs a computation the compiler moves to lower precision (design principles 7.2-7.3), and reading it back into a wider type is exact. A kernel that computes in f32 only refuses the other element types at the call, naming the operator, the argument type and the set it takes, instead of narrowing. f32 programs keep their bytes.
